.TH HOMELAB-CLI-MAIL 8 "2026-08-21" "homelab-cli" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
homelab-cli\-mail \- Mail (IMAP) commands for homelab\-cli
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B homelab-cli
.RB [ --api-url
.IR url ]
.B mail
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B homelab-cli mail
groups mail/IMAP-related REST calls under one command family. These talk
to the mail server as the logged-in account \- no separate mail password
is ever needed, since homelab-api authenticates to Dovecot using the same
JWT this CLI already holds.
.P
A message's content is exposed as a flat list of MIME parts (see
.BR message ")"
\- there is no single "body" field, since a message may have a
plain-text part, an HTML part, an encrypted payload, or something else
entirely, and it isn't this tool's job to guess which one to show. Use
.B part
to fetch any specific part's actual content by its part number.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Show the current INBOX unseen and total message counts.
.TP
.B folders
List mail folders/mailboxes, including special-use flags (\\Sent,
\\Trash, \\Drafts, \\Archive, \\Junk) when the server reports them.
.TP
.BI messages " [--folder NAME] [--offset N] [--limit N]"
List messages in a folder, newest first, headers only (UID, flags,
subject, from, date, size).
.I --folder
defaults to INBOX;
.I --limit
defaults to 50 and is capped at 200. Pagination is computed from the
folder's message count at request time, not a stable cursor \- a page can
shift slightly if mail arrives or is expunged between two calls.
.TP
.BI message " UID [--folder NAME]"
Show one message's headers (date, subject, from, to, cc, message-id), its
flat list of MIME parts (part number, content-type, charset, filename,
size, disposition), and the raw, unparsed header block as base64 in
.I rawheaderb64
\- no part content is included. Use
.B part
to download a specific part's actual content.
.TP
.BI part " UID PART [--folder NAME] [--output PATH]"
Download one MIME part's decoded content by its part number (e.g.
.RI \(lq 1 \(rq
or
.RI \(lq 1.2 \(rq,
as shown by
.BR message ")."
Works the same way whether that part is a plain-text body, an HTML body,
or a binary attachment.
.TP
.BI search " [--folder NAME] [criteria...] [--limit N]"
Search messages in a folder, newest first, same result shape as
.BR messages "."
Criteria:
.BR --from ", " --to ", " --subject ", " --body
(substring match),
.BR --since ", " --before
(date, YYYY-MM-DD),
.BR --unseen ", " --seen ", " --flagged ", " --unflagged
(boolean). Multiple criteria are ANDed together.
.TP
.BI flags " UID [--folder NAME] [--add FLAG]... [--remove FLAG]..."
Add or remove flags on a message (e.g.
.RI \(lq \\eSeen \(rq
to mark read,
.RI \(lq \\eFlagged \(rq
to star it). At least one
.B --add
or
.B --remove
is required. Returns the message's full flag list after the change.
.TP
.BI move " UID TO [--folder NAME]"
Move a message to another folder.
.I --folder
(source) defaults to INBOX.
.TP
.BI trash " UID [--folder NAME]"
Move a message to the account's Trash folder \- the folder marked
.B \eTrash
via SPECIAL-USE, resolved automatically, never guessed. Recoverable: the
message still exists, just relocated. Fails if the account has no folder
(or more than one) marked
.BR \eTrash "."
.TP
.BI purge " UID"
Permanently delete a message.
.B Irreversible.
Only works on a message already sitting in the account's Trash folder \-
there is deliberately no
.B --folder
option, since this command can never target a live mailbox. Use
.B trash
first, then
.B purge
the same UID once it's actually in Trash.
.TP
.BI folder-create " NAME"
Create a new mail folder.
.TP
.BI folder-rename " NAME TO"
Rename a mail folder. Refuses INBOX and any folder carrying a
SPECIAL-USE flag (Trash, Sent, Drafts, Archive, Junk).
.TP
.BI folder-delete " NAME"
Delete a mail folder. Same special-use-folder refusal as
.BR folder-rename "."
.TP
.B allowed-from
List which specific addresses, and which whole domains (wildcard), the
current account is authorized to send mail as. Purely a display of
Postfix's own sender-login enforcement \- not itself the enforcement
point.
.TP
.BI send " --from FROM --to TO... --subject SUBJECT [--cc TO]... [--bcc TO]... [--body TEXT] [--html HTML] [--attach-file PATH]... [--attach-drive ID]... [--in-reply-to FOLDER:UID]"
Compose and send a message via SMTP, then best-effort file a copy into
the account's Sent folder (a failure to file the Sent copy is reported as
a
.I warning
on an otherwise successful response \- the message has already
irreversibly been sent, so this never fails the whole call).
.I --from
must be an address or domain already listed by
.BR allowed-from ";"
an unauthorized
.I --from
is rejected before any SMTP connection is even opened.
.I --to
,
.I --cc
, and
.I --bcc
are each repeatable.
.I --attach-file
uploads a local file fresh (repeatable);
.I --attach-drive
attaches an existing Drive file by its numeric
.B id
(as shown by
.BR homelab-cli-drive (8)'s
.IR list-files ", not its "
.IR uuid ";"
repeatable). Passing a directory id to
.I --attach-drive
is rejected \- attaching an entire directory is not yet implemented.
.I --in-reply-to
threads this message as a reply to an existing message (sets
In-Reply-To/References), given as
.IR FOLDER:UID ,
e.g.
.IR INBOX:1234 .
.TP
.BI draft " [same flags as send]"
Builds the same MIME message as
.B send
but appends it to the account's Drafts folder instead of transmitting
it.
.I --to
and
.I --subject
are optional here, since a draft may be incomplete.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR homelab-cli (8),
.BR homelab-cli-drive (8),
.BR homelab-cli-admin (8)
